name: permissions and identity
boss: Gatekeeper
flavor: Every door has a lock. I hold the modes.
brief: Every file has three sets of permissions -- owner, group, everyone --
brief: and three bits in each: read, write, execute. ls -l shows them as
brief: rwxr-xr-x. chmod changes them.
brief:
brief: Numeric modes are the fast way once they click: r=4, w=2, x=1, added up
brief: per set. 755 is rwxr-xr-x, 644 is rw-r--r--, 600 is rw------- .
brief:
brief: On xos everything runs as root and the root filesystem is read-only, so
brief: these matter for what you write to tmpfs and to disks you mount -- not
brief: for defending this machine from you.
---
q: %make% @script@ executable
a: chmod +x @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: +x adds execute for everyone at once, owner group and other
---
q: {set @script@ to mode 755 -- everyone may read and run, only you may write|use chmod 755 on @script@ so anyone can read and run it, only you can write|set @script@'s mode to 755, giving read+execute to all and write to just you}
a: chmod 755 @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 7=rwx, 5=r-x -- add read(4)+write(2)+execute(1) per set
---
q: {set @txt@ to mode 644|use chmod 644 on @txt@ so you get read+write and everyone else gets read-only|set @txt@'s mode to 644}
a: chmod 644 @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 6=rw-, 4=r-- -- you get read+write, everyone else read-only
---
q: {set @txt@ so only you can read or write it, nobody else anything|use chmod 600 on @txt@ to lock out everyone but you|set @txt@'s mode to 600, all access removed from group and other}
a: chmod 600 @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 6=rw-, 0=--- -- nothing at all for group or other
---
q: {remove the execute bit from @script@ for everyone|use chmod -x on @script@ to strip execute from every set|take execute permission off @script@ entirely}
a: chmod -x @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: a bare -x with no letter in front strips execute from all three sets
---
q: {add write permission for the group on @txt@|use chmod g+w on @txt@ to give the group write access|grant the group write permission on @txt@, nothing else changed}
a: chmod g+w @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: g targets the group set specifically, leaving owner and other untouched
---
q: {take away all permissions from everyone-else on @txt@|use chmod o= on @txt@ to zero out the other set|strip all permissions from everyone but owner and group on @txt@}
a: chmod o= @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: = sets the listed permissions exactly, and an empty list means none
---
q: %make% every file under @dir@ readable by everyone, recursively
a: chmod -R a+r @dir@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod -R
hint: -R walks into every subdirectory and applies the change there too
---
q: {who are you|ask the shell who you are logged in as|print your current username}
a: whoami
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: whoami
teach: whoami
hint: whoami is a shortcut for id -un
---
q: %show% your user id, group id and groups all at once
a: id
out: uid=
ref: id
teach: id
hint: id with no flags prints uid, gid and every group at once
---
q: %show% just your numeric user id
a: id -u
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -u
hint: -u limits output to just the numeric uid
---
q: %show% just your user NAME rather than the number
a: id -un
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: id
teach: id -n
hint: -n added to -u prints the name instead of the number
---
q: %show% just your numeric group id
a: id -g
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -g
hint: -g limits output to just the numeric gid
---
q: %show% every group you belong to, numerically
a: id -G
out: [0-9]
ref: id
teach: id -G
hint: -G lists every group id you belong to, not just the primary
---
q: %show% your real user id rather than the effective one
a: id -ur
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -r
hint: -r combined with -u asks for the real id rather than the effective one
---
q: %show% the default permission mask new files are created with
a: umask
out: [0-7]
ref: umask
teach: umask
hint: umask alone prints the current mask without changing it
---
q: {set the mask so new files are private to you|use umask 077 so new files start out owner-only|set the creation mask to 077}
a: umask 077
ref: umask
teach: umask
hint: umask is subtracted from the default; 077 leaves only owner permissions
---
q: %show% everything the filesystem knows about @txt@
a: stat @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat
hint: stat alone prints every field it knows about a file
---
q: %show% just the size of @txt@ in bytes, nothing else
a: stat -c %s @txt@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
hint: -c takes a format; %s is size, %n name, %a permission bits
---
q: %show% the permission bits of @script@ as a number
a: stat -c %a @script@
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
hint: %a in the format string is permission bits
---
q: %show% filesystem information for @txt@ rather than the file itself
a: stat -f @txt@
out: ID|Block
ref: stat
teach: stat -f
hint: -f switches from the file's info to the filesystem it lives on
---
q: {stat the target of a symlink rather than the link|use stat -L to report on what a symlink points to, not the link|follow a symlink with stat -L before reading its info}
a: stat -L @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat -L
hint: -L follows the link and reports on its target instead of the link itself
---
q: %show% stat's output in terse single-line form
a: stat -t @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: stat
teach: stat -t
hint: -t packs every field onto one line, script-friendly
---
q: {set @txt@'s timestamp to a date you name rather than to now|use touch -d to stamp @txt@ with a date you choose|give @txt@ an explicit timestamp instead of the current time}
a: touch -d 2024-03-01 @txt@
ref: touch
teach: touch -d
hint: -d takes an explicit date/time instead of using now
---
q: {give @txt2@ the same timestamp @txt@ already has|use touch -r to copy @txt@'s timestamp onto @txt2@|stamp @txt2@ with whatever timestamp @txt@ has}
a: touch -r @txt@ @txt2@
ref: touch
teach: touch -r
hint: -r copies another file's timestamp instead of specifying one yourself
